ince 2000, Crescent City has set <strong class=\"sig\">8 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 79 that chance alone would give, and <strong>30 record highs</strong>, far fewer than the 80 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (109). Crescent City's all-time high is 97\u00b0F, on September 20, 1919.","t_chg":"Crescent City's nights got warmer while its days cooled","p_chg":"Across 105 complete years: Crescent City's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.21\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.16 to +0.27), about +2.8\u00b0F since 1895. Crescent City's average high has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.25\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.36 to \u22120.14), about \u22123.2\u00b0F since 1895. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it.","t_mon":"Lows rose in all 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months; 9 months fell.","t_rain":"Crescent City has gotten drier","p_rain":"Crescent City's yearly precipitation has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22121.79 inches per decade</strong> (95% range \u22122.69 to \u22120.90), about \u221222.9 inches since 1897. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 61.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1904 (107.61 in); the driest, 2013 (28.92 in). Crescent City almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 1 daily record high, no daily record lows"}}